From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephen Bates <sbates@raithlin.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:29:07 -0500
Subject: [RFC PATCH 8/8] ionic: add queue management ops and allow unreadable Rx payload

With these two pieces a dma-buf can be bound to an ionic Rx queue
(NETDEV_CMD_BIND_RX) once tcp-data-split is on.

The payload page pool of a header split queue gets
PP_FLAG_ALLOW_UNREADABLE_NETMEM and its queue index, so that it picks
up the memory provider of its queue. The header pool does not. All the
places that create Rx queues run under the netdev instance lock since
the first patch of this series, as the page pool requires.

The queue management ops are a stub. ionic has no command to take one
queue down and back up while the others run: there is Q_CONTROL to
disable and enable a queue and Q_INIT to set it up again, but that has
only ever been done for all queues at once, between a stop and a start
of the whole LIF, and Rx queue i shares its interrupt and NAPI with Tx
queue i. Whether firmware allows it for one queue of a running LIF is a
question for the firmware team. Until then, restart everything, the way
a ring size change does:

 - ndo_queue_mem_alloc() builds a second copy of the Rx queue's rings
   and page pools, which is where the new pool finds the provider.

 - ndo_queue_stop() stops and cleans all the queues.

 - ndo_queue_start() swaps the new rings in and starts all the queues,
   before it returns: ionic_start_queues_reconfig() leaves the start to
   the link check work, which would run after the bind was reported as
   done. If the start fails it swaps the old rings back in. Either way
   it attaches the netdev again, as ionic_start_queues_reconfig() does:
   if the core's second try with the old rings fails as well, the
   queues are down but a down and up of the netdev can still bring
   them back.

 - The old rings never leave the lif, so there is nothing for
   ndo_queue_stop() to hand back; ndo_queue_start() frees them once the
   queues run. A queue memory that holds nothing means "what the lif
   has", which is what the core passes back to ndo_queue_start() when it
   has to undo a failed start.

This is correct, but a bind or unbind interrupts all traffic on the
netdev and drops what was in flight on the Tx queues, as a ring size
change does.

The queues only exist while the netdev is up, so
ndo_queue_mem_alloc() fails with -ENETDOWN when it is down or the
firmware is being reset. A binding that exists when the netdev goes
down is picked up again when the queues are made on the way up. A
binding can't be made while it is down, which is what bnxt does as
well.

IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET is set before the reset paths take the instance
lock, so a reset can begin in the middle of a bind or unbind:

 - ndo_queue_start() that finds the flag set leaves the new rings in
   the lif and reports success without touching the device. The reset
   is waiting for the lock to free all the queues, and the recovery
   makes them again from the provider the queue has by then.

 - ionic_restart_lif() now clears the flag before it drops the
   instance lock, not after. An unbind in between found live queues
   with the flag set, was told -ENETDOWN, which the core takes to mean
   that there is nothing to undo, and left the queue on a provider the
   core had already forgotten.

ionic_swap_queues() used to point the page pools that go to the
temporary queue at that queue's NAPI. The temporary queue is freed
right after its pools are destroyed, and a softirq that returns a
buffer of such a pool reads the pool's NAPI to decide whether it may
recycle directly: with an unbind there are buffers still out in
sockets. Give those pools no NAPI instead; the temporary queue has
none that could run. This also covers the ring and channel changes,
which had the same window.

The dma-buf has to be large enough. A header split queue wants one page
per descriptor, more with an MTU above a page, for as long as the ring
is full, on top of what the application holds. With less the ring is
only partly filled and rx_N_alloc_err counts; the previous patch keeps
the queue receiving with what it has, and looking for buffers when it
has none.

All of this runs without rtnl_lock. I found nothing in the reconfigure
path that needs it, but until now it never ran without.
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_lif.c | 207 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 200 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_lif.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_lif.c
index 14175acf0000..b35064fc5280 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_lif.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/pensando/ionic/ionic_lif.c
@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@
 #include <linux/crash_dump.h>
 #include <linux/vmalloc.h>
 #include <net/netdev_lock.h>
+#include <net/netdev_queues.h>
 #include <net/page_pool/helpers.h>
 
 #include "ionic.h"
@@ -575,7 +576,7 @@ err_out:
 }
 
 static int ionic_rxq_pools_create(struct ionic_lif *lif, struct ionic_qcq *qcq,
-				  unsigned int num_descs,
+				  unsigned int index, unsigned int num_descs,
 				  struct bpf_prog *xdp_prog)
 {
 	struct page_pool_params pp_params = {
@@ -589,11 +590,22 @@ static int ionic_rxq_pools_create(struct ionic_lif *lif, struct ionic_qcq *qcq,
 		.max_len = PAGE_SIZE,
 		.netdev = lif->netdev,
 	};
+	bool hds = qcq->q.features & IONIC_RXQ_F_HDS;
 	struct page_pool *pool;
 
 	if (xdp_prog)
 		pp_params.dma_dir = DMA_BIDIRECTIONAL;
 
+	/* Only header split keeps the driver, and with the checks in
+	 * ionic_rx_clean() the stack, out of the payload buffers, so only
+	 * then may they come from a memory provider.  The pool looks up the
+	 * provider of its queue, under the netdev instance lock.
+	 */
+	if (hds) {
+		pp_params.flags |= PP_FLAG_ALLOW_UNREADABLE_NETMEM;
+		pp_params.queue_idx = index;
+	}
+
 	pool = page_pool_create(&pp_params);
 	if (IS_ERR(pool)) {
 		netdev_err(lif->netdev, "Cannot create page_pool\n");
@@ -601,12 +613,14 @@ static int ionic_rxq_pools_create(struct ionic_lif *lif, struct ionic_qcq *qcq,
 	}
 	qcq->q.page_pool = pool;
 
-	if (!(qcq->q.features & IONIC_RXQ_F_HDS))
+	if (!hds)
 		return 0;
 
 	/* The headers are copied out of their buffers, which are small, so
-	 * this pool only ever holds a few pages.
+	 * this pool holds one page for every 16 descriptors.  They have to
+	 * be readable.
 	 */
+	pp_params.flags &= ~PP_FLAG_ALLOW_UNREADABLE_NETMEM;
 	pp_params.pool_size = DIV_ROUND_UP(num_descs,
 					   PAGE_SIZE / IONIC_HDS_HDR_BUF_LEN);
 
@@ -658,7 +672,8 @@ static int ionic_qcq_alloc(struct ionic_lif *lif, unsigned int type,
 	}
 
 	if (type == IONIC_QTYPE_RXQ) {
-		err = ionic_rxq_pools_create(lif, new, num_descs, xdp_prog);
+		err = ionic_rxq_pools_create(lif, new, index, num_descs,
+					     xdp_prog);
 		if (err)
 			goto err_out_free_q_info;
 	}
@@ -2967,22 +2982,30 @@ err_out:
 	return err;
 }
 
+/* Swap the rings of a queue of the lif, a, with those of a temporary one, b,
+ * while both are stopped.
+ */
 static void ionic_swap_queues(struct ionic_qcq *a, struct ionic_qcq *b)
 {
 	/* only swapping the queues and napi, not flags or other stuff */
 	swap(a->napi,         b->napi);
 
 	if (a->q.type == IONIC_QTYPE_RXQ) {
+		/* The pools that go to the temporary queue get no NAPI.  It
+		 * has none that could run, and it is freed with no grace
+		 * period for a softirq that is returning a buffer and reads
+		 * the pool's NAPI to see if it may recycle directly.
+		 */
 		swap(a->q.page_pool, b->q.page_pool);
 		a->q.page_pool->p.napi = &a->napi;
 		if (b->q.page_pool)  /* is NULL when increasing queue count */
-			b->q.page_pool->p.napi = &b->napi;
+			WRITE_ONCE(b->q.page_pool->p.napi, NULL);
 
 		swap(a->q.hdr_pool, b->q.hdr_pool);
 		if (a->q.hdr_pool)
 			a->q.hdr_pool->p.napi = &a->napi;
 		if (b->q.hdr_pool)
-			b->q.hdr_pool->p.napi = &b->napi;
+			WRITE_ONCE(b->q.hdr_pool->p.napi, NULL);
 	}
 
 	swap(a->q.features,   b->q.features);
@@ -3270,6 +3293,170 @@ err_out:
 	return err;
 }
 
+/* Queue management ops, by way of every queue.
+ *
+ * The device has no command to take one queue down and back up while the
+ * others run, and Rx queue i shares its interrupt and NAPI with Tx queue i.
+ * So until there is a way to restart one queue, do what a ring size change
+ * does: build the new Rx queue next to the old one, stop all the queues,
+ * swap the two, and start all the queues again.
+ *
+ * This means that the memory of the stopped queue is not handed back through
+ * ndo_queue_stop().  The old rings stay in the lif until ndo_queue_start()
+ * swaps the new ones in, and it frees them itself once the queues are
+ * running again, or puts them back if they are not.  A struct ionic_queue_mem
+ * that holds no queue stands for "what the lif has".
+ *
+ * A firmware reset frees the queues and its recovery makes them again, from
+ * the memory provider each queue has by then.  IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET is set
+ * outside of the instance lock, so it can turn up between any two of the
+ * callbacks.
+ *
+ * The netdev instance lock is held, but RTNL is not.
+ */
+struct ionic_queue_mem {
+	struct ionic_qcq *qcq;
+};
+
+static void ionic_queue_mem_free(struct net_device *netdev,
+				 void *per_queue_mem)
+{
+	struct ionic_queue_mem *qmem = per_queue_mem;
+	struct ionic_lif *lif = netdev_priv(netdev);
+
+	if (!qmem->qcq)
+		return;
+
+	/* never had an interrupt of its own */
+	qmem->qcq->flags &= ~IONIC_QCQ_F_INTR;
+	ionic_qcq_free(lif, qmem->qcq);
+	devm_kfree(lif->ionic->dev, qmem->qcq);
+	qmem->qcq = NULL;
+}
+
+static int ionic_queue_mem_alloc(struct net_device *netdev,
+				 struct netdev_queue_config *qcfg,
+				 void *per_queue_mem, int idx)
+{
+	struct ionic_queue_mem *qmem = per_queue_mem;
+	struct ionic_lif *lif = netdev_priv(netdev);
+	unsigned int comp_sz, flags;
+	int err;
+
+	/* The queues, and with them their page pools, only exist while the
+	 * netdev is up and the firmware is there.  They are made again from
+	 * the queue's current memory provider when that changes.
+	 */
+	if (!netif_running(netdev) ||
+	    test_bit(IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET, lif->state))
+		return -ENETDOWN;
+
+	/* no room in the CMB for a second copy of the rings */
+	if (test_bit(IONIC_LIF_F_CMB_RX_RINGS, lif->state))
+		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+
+	mutex_lock(&lif->queue_lock);
+
+	if (!lif->rxqcqs || !lif->rxqcqs[idx]) {
+		err = -ENETDOWN;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
+
+	comp_sz = sizeof(struct ionic_rxq_comp);
+	if (lif->rxq_features & IONIC_Q_F_2X_CQ_DESC)
+		comp_sz *= 2;
+
+	flags = lif->rxqcqs[idx]->flags & ~IONIC_QCQ_F_INTR;
+	err = ionic_qcq_alloc(lif, IONIC_QTYPE_RXQ, idx, "rx", flags,
+			      lif->nrxq_descs, sizeof(struct ionic_rxq_desc),
+			      comp_sz, sizeof(struct ionic_rxq_sg_desc),
+			      sizeof(struct ionic_rx_desc_info),
+			      lif->kern_pid, lif->xdp_prog,
+			      lif->rxq_features, &qmem->qcq);
+	if (!err)
+		qmem->qcq->q.xdp_prog = lif->xdp_prog;
+
+out_unlock:
+	mutex_unlock(&lif->queue_lock);
+
+	return err;
+}
+
+static int ionic_queue_stop(struct net_device *netdev, void *per_queue_mem,
+			    int idx)
+{
+	struct ionic_lif *lif = netdev_priv(netdev);
+
+	mutex_lock(&lif->queue_lock);
+	ionic_stop_queues_reconfig(lif);
+	mutex_unlock(&lif->queue_lock);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int ionic_queue_start(struct net_device *netdev,
+			     struct netdev_queue_config *qcfg,
+			     void *per_queue_mem, int idx)
+{
+	struct ionic_queue_mem *qmem = per_queue_mem;
+	struct ionic_lif *lif = netdev_priv(netdev);
+	int err;
+
+	mutex_lock(&lif->queue_lock);
+
+	if (qmem->qcq)
+		ionic_swap_queues(lif->rxqcqs[idx], qmem->qcq);
+
+	/* A firmware reset began after the queues were stopped and waits
+	 * for the instance lock to free them.  Its recovery makes them again
+	 * for the provider the queue has now, so this is as good as done.
+	 * The netdev stays detached until then.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET, lif->state)) {
+		ionic_queue_mem_free(netdev, qmem);
+		mutex_unlock(&lif->queue_lock);
+		return 0;
+	}
+
+	/* Unlike ionic_start_queues_reconfig() this can't leave the start to
+	 * the link check: that would run after the caller has been told
+	 * that the queue is up.
+	 */
+	err = ionic_txrx_init(lif);
+	if (!err && netif_carrier_ok(netdev)) {
+		err = ionic_start_queues(lif);
+		if (err)
+			ionic_txrx_deinit(lif);
+	}
+
+	if (err) {
+		/* give the lif its rings back, for another try */
+		if (qmem->qcq)
+			ionic_swap_queues(lif->rxqcqs[idx], qmem->qcq);
+	} else {
+		/* now holds the rings that were stopped */
+		ionic_queue_mem_free(netdev, qmem);
+	}
+
+	/* This was detached in ionic_queue_stop().  As after a failed
+	 * ionic_start_queues_reconfig(), attach it even if the queues did
+	 * not start, so that a down and up can try again.
+	 */
+	netif_device_attach(netdev);
+
+	mutex_unlock(&lif->queue_lock);
+
+	return err;
+}
+
+static const struct netdev_queue_mgmt_ops ionic_queue_mgmt_ops = {
+	.ndo_queue_mem_size	= sizeof(struct ionic_queue_mem),
+	.ndo_queue_mem_alloc	= ionic_queue_mem_alloc,
+	.ndo_queue_mem_free	= ionic_queue_mem_free,
+	.ndo_queue_start	= ionic_queue_start,
+	.ndo_queue_stop		= ionic_queue_stop,
+};
+
 static int ionic_affinity_masks_alloc(struct ionic *ionic)
 {
 	cpumask_var_t *affinity_masks;
@@ -3336,6 +3523,7 @@ int ionic_lif_alloc(struct ionic *ionic)
 	ionic->lif = lif;
 	lif->ionic = ionic;
 	netdev->netdev_ops = &ionic_netdev_ops;
+	netdev->queue_mgmt_ops = &ionic_queue_mgmt_ops;
 	netdev->request_ops_lock = true;
 	ionic_ethtool_set_ops(netdev);
 
@@ -3533,10 +3721,15 @@ int ionic_restart_lif(struct ionic_lif *lif)
 			goto err_txrx_free;
 	}
 
+	/* The queues exist again.  Say so before the lock is dropped: a
+	 * queue op that finds them with the reset flag still set would leave
+	 * them as they are.
+	 */
+	clear_bit(IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET, lif->state);
+
 	mutex_unlock(&lif->queue_lock);
 	netdev_unlock(lif->netdev);
 
-	clear_bit(IONIC_LIF_F_FW_RESET, lif->state);
 	ionic_link_status_check_request(lif, CAN_SLEEP);
 	netif_device_attach(lif->netdev);
 	ionic_queue_doorbell_check(ionic, IONIC_NAPI_DEADLINE);
-- 
